# nscale CLI environment profiles, read by bin/nsc.
#
# Copy the .env.example files in this directory to
# ~/.config/nscale/profiles.d/<name>.env and fill in the real values. The real
# files are NOT tracked: they name internal organizations, projects and regions,
# and this repository is public. Only the shape lives here.
#
# Keys (every one optional; an omitted key means "not applicable"):
#
#   NSC_DESC              One line describing the environment. Shown by
#                         `nsc list` and in the confirmation prompt.
#   NSC_ENV               Value for $ENV, which is what selects the CLI's
#                         endpoint set (cmd/config.go). Leave EMPTY for
#                         production: an absent ENV is what production means.
#   NSC_USER              Keyring slot for credentials, passed as --user. Give
#                         every environment its own: the slot is keyed on this
#                         name alone, with no environment in the key, and the
#                         feature-flag and completion caches are keyed the same
#                         way. Sharing a slot across environments overwrites
#                         one's refresh token with the other's and mixes up
#                         their cached completions. Use "default" for
#                         production only.
#   NSC_CONTEXT           Context name passed as --context, holding the
#                         org/project/region IDs. Create it with
#                         `nscale contexts set`. Always named explicitly, so
#                         contexts.yaml's `current:` stays cleared and can
#                         never point one environment's IDs at another's URLs.
#   NSC_BIN               Override the nscale binary for this profile. Only
#                         needed for a --force-k8s-flag build; leave unset.
#   NSC_VERCEL_BYPASS_OP  1Password reference (op://...) for the Vercel
#                         deployment-protection bypass secret, needed by any
#                         environment whose console is behind Vercel SSO. Read
#                         at runtime and cached in the per-login tmpfs, never
#                         written to disk. One secret covers dev and staging.
#   NSC_TOKEN_FILE        Path to a service-account token file, for headless
#                         use where no device flow is possible. Read on every
#                         invocation so a rotation needs no action here.
#                         Mutually exclusive with NSC_USER in practice: a
#                         service token short-circuits login entirely.
#   NSC_CONFIRM           1 asks before any command that is not plainly a read.
#                         Worth it for an environment holding real customer
#                         infrastructure.
